# Target C — two-phase loaded-hull side charge

**Date:** 2026-09-13  
**Status:** proof-only conditional candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The two-phase boundary charge can be strengthened using the Type-II loaded-hull
normalization. In a globally phase-pure two-direction graph, only four loaded-hull
sides can use the selected directions; every other loaded-hull unit side is paid by a
nonquadrilateral face or by an outer bridge.

## 1. Finite side-direction bound

Let `F` be a convex polygon with all vertices extreme, and fix two projective line
directions `[u]` and `[v]`. At most two sides of `F` have projective direction `[u]`:
the two supporting lines parallel to that direction. A third parallel side would lie
strictly between those supporting lines and could not contain an extreme vertex.
The same holds for `[v]`, so at most four sides use either selected direction.

## 2. Loaded-hull charge

Work in the Type-II normalization `c_B=0`, where every loaded-loaded unit contact is a
side of the loaded force hull `F`. Let `e_BB` count these contacts. Assume every
bounded quadrilateral contact face is a strict rhombus using only `[u]` or `[v]`.

Among the `e_BB` loaded-hull sides, at most four are phase-aligned. Any remaining side
is either an outer-face bridge or a nonbridge edge with a bounded face on its other
side. In the latter case that face cannot be a phase-pure quadrilateral, so it is a
bounded nonquadrilateral face. Charging once per physical side gives

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}\le4+P_{exc}+B_o,}
\tag{LH.1}
\]

where `P_exc=4f+Q` is the total degree of bounded nonquadrilateral faces and `B_o`
counts outer bridges as physical edges. The charge is injective on nonbridge sides;
the bridge term is counted once even though an outer bridge has two outer-walk
occurrences.

Using the canonical Section-123 payment gives

\[
e_{BB}\le4+10(e_{BB}-k)+B_o,
\qquad k=d_H+s_Z.
\tag{LH.2}
\]

Using `e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B` and `P_{exc}=4f+Q` gives the equivalent form

\[
\boxed{2k+u_B\le4f+B_o+4.}
\tag{LH.3}
\]

## 3. Composition with bridge-subtracted area

In the full-core Type-II near-disk branch, the bridge-subtracted rhombus-area bound
gives

\[
2B_o\le(b-\pi D)+P_{exc}+o(D).
\tag{LH.4}
\]

Substituting (LH.4) into (LH.1) yields the all-detour source-level relation

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}
\le4+{3\over2}P_{exc}+{1\over2}(b-\pi D)+o(D).
}
\tag{LH.5}
\]

Thus a two-phase full-core survivor cannot have simultaneously large loaded-loaded
side population and both small exceptional-face perimeter and small outer detour.
The relation is one-way: it supplies no upper bound on `e_BB`, `P_exc`, or the outer
detour separately.

The audited Type-II all-defect gap supplies a proof-only lower bound
`e_{BB}/D >= 0.1669-o(1)` in its stated scope. Combining it with (LH.5) forces a
linear route surplus in the two-phase branch, but the existing global
`0.1224D-o(D)` route floor and the high-detour scalar regression show that this is
still compatible with the current necessary region.

## 4. Scope and classification

- The phase-purity hypothesis is global for the bounded quadrilateral faces of the
  graph under discussion; corridor purity alone is insufficient.
- `c_B=0` is the canonical Type-II loaded-contact theorem. Type I is untouched.
- Contact-isolated source points remain retained and are not put into the core stress
  matrix.
- No conclusion about KKT weights, global optimality of a constructed family, a strict
  diameter decrease, or a unit equilateral triangle is made.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting hostile audit; proof-only. C-II remains
open.
